# Preserve original audio, cinematics, localization, UI, mission, and tuning…

Preserve original audio, cinematics, localization, UI, mission, and tuning
data in deterministic normalized forms.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Preserve original audio, cinematics, localization, UI, mission, and tuning
  data in deterministic normalized forms; keep English as canonical base input
  and preserve other official languages as deterministic language-mod sources.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

The faithful extraction pipeline remains nine base-only stages. It normalizes
RSD audio to WAV, extracts RCF archives, and converts supported RMV/Bink movies
to HAP video plus one PCM WAV per source audio stream. Movie stream order is
preserved as `audio_track_01.wav`, `audio_track_02.wav`, and so on; the current
normalized movie tree contains 16 English track-01 packages and eight packages
each carrying localized tracks 02, 03, and 04.

Canonical non-English localization is now internal Rust product code under
`src/localization/languages/`. The original PC/PAL runtime evidence maps
French/German/Spanish
FMV audio indexes 1/2/3 to normalized tracks 02/03/04 after the English index-0
track. Language composition also preserves the exact TextBible table/sidecar,
matching dialogue RCF, localized loading/license UI assets, optional localized
readme, SHA-256 evidence, and UTF-8 text records. The current lawful source has
21 localized loading/license assets for each of French/German/Spanish and real
dialogue archives for all three. Eleven external Rust integration tests cover
all three cinematic-track mappings, fail-closed missing UI/dialogue/cinematic
audio, fail-closed Italian placeholder-only source, source-root read-only
containment, byte-deterministic repeated output, normalized mod-package
publication, and manifest/report serialization agreement.

Italian remains intentionally unavailable from the current source: all 1,632
Italian text records are `???`, `dialogi.rcf` is absent, and no Italian
localized
loading/license assets are present. No content is synthesized to fill those
gaps.

The shared normalized SHAR mod-package contract now backs canonical language
publication directly, and the language domain is separated from filesystem,
serialization, hashing, and package-publication composition. Language mods are
not inserted as a tenth faithful-base extraction stage and do not alter
base-game identity.

Mission and tuning preservation now also reaches the atomic `prepare-unreal`
staging boundary. The current normalized corpus carries 255 `vehicle-tuning`
sources, and each verified source produces one canonical versioned tuning core
that preserves source statements, command order, raw argument lexemes, route
provenance, and exact physical-vehicle provenance when it resolves
unambiguously. Mission-local `SetCarAttributes` and reviewed stage AI/catch-up
tuples publish separately with exact arguments, source ordinals, mission/stage/
objective ownership, and physical vehicle provenance.

Contextual mission commands that select `.con` tuning profiles publish as a
third versioned bundle;
an unavailable source-backed profile remains explicit `null` rather than being
repaired or guessed. Bundle validators replay source order, command arguments,
ownership, physical bindings, and contextual usage evidence before publication.

The accepted staging snapshot contains 154 mission-definition rows, 173
mission-local tuning rows, 255 source-backed tuning cores, and 152 contextual
tuning usages. Of those contextual usages, 151 resolve one exact tuning source
and one preserves its authored unresolved path as `null`; the tuning cores bind
88 exact physical vehicles and retain 167 explicit unbound cores. Mission-local
and contextual occurrence identities are unique by source plus authored ordinal,
and the vehicle-tuning core validator now independently replays route, original
byte count, statements, and every normalized command field before publication.

Mission definitions likewise retain their typed source report through staging;
the publication gate rerenders each complete definition and rejects any wire
field that differs. Selection, completeness, source order, and exact replay now
derive from that single typed ledger rather than a parallel source-id list.

Release integration now binds the semantic sidecars persistently. The published
plan index v4 carries a fixed identity, sibling filename, exact byte count, and
SHA-256 revision for `mission-definitions.jsonl`, `mission-tuning.jsonl`,
`vehicle-tuning.jsonl`, and `vehicle-tuning-usage.jsonl`; those bindings
participate in the release-index revision. The downstream local reader requires
all four regular non-linked siblings and verifies their exact size and digest
before accepting the plans. Synthetic tests reject both a missing sidecar and a
same-size byte mutation.

Corpus-level v4 publication evidence remains pending
until an exclusive `prepare-unreal` run completes with the current producer.

These preservation artifacts intentionally do not map legacy setters to
gameplay units, native Unreal fields, conversion rules, or runtime ranges. This
parent therefore remains open for reviewed native tuning/mission compilation and
final release integration evidence.
